TEXT -576 248 Left 2 !.param vfactor=sqrt(3) sfactor=3
TEXT -576 264 Left 2 !.options vntol=1m abstol=1m
TEXT -576 296 Left 2 !.op
TEXT -944 112 Left 2 ;This is a high energy network example to show you some of the loadflow devices.\nPlace the symbols in lib/sym/loadflow. The necessary library files are ltspice.lib and ltspice_special.lib\nDevices are : Voltage or current slacks , transformers , lines, impedances, loads, .....
TEXT -944 152 Left 2 ;General: Each network element has at least two inputs/outputs : one represent a real quantity ( voltage , power , ..) and one a imag quantity\nThe real pin is signed with a "x" \nYou should only connect "x" pins with "x" pins and pins without "x" with pins without "x"\nThe results of the loadflow analysis ( with .op or .tran analysis ) are the "normal" spice variables ( current,voltage) and \nthe variables from the additional measurement nodes of each device ( real , imag power, voltage magnitudes and so on )
TEXT -616 72 Left 3 ;This example shows the optimal set for the transformer tap ( parameter svar=phase regulation ) to minimize the powerlost of the two lines and  the transformer
TEXT 144 392 Left 2 ;Additional measurement nodes for magnitude of line to line voltage, real power, imag power\nUnfortunately the unit is always "V" because the computation is made by voltage sources
TEXT 88 848 Left 2 ;Here you can edit model parameters \nFor instance you can switch on/off the element or change the actual\nadditional voltage setting ( l_ and s_  parameter ) of the tranformer to control\npower flow and the voltage
TEXT 144 808 Left 2 ;Here you can change the length of the line_LF ( unit is km )
TEXT 352 712 Left 2 ;vr = real part of voltage \nvi = imag part
TEXT -208 832 Left 2 ;p = real power in Watt\nq = imag power in Watt \nis p neg. -> Generator\nis p pos -> Load \nis q pos -> inductive Load or cappacitive Generator
TEXT -248 104 Left 2 ;Current slack with constant real an imag parts ir,ii
TEXT -104 200 Left 2 ;ln1_p1 means real power on port 1 ( input ) \npos. sign is always to the port ! \nln1_p2 means real power on port2 ( output )\nIf power flows into port 1 and out of port 2 : p1 = pos , p2 = neg , difference is power lost \nof the device
TEXT -288 424 Left 2 ;connection line voltages are always\n line to earth ( nominal voltage / vfactor )
TEXT 168 672 Left 2 ;Power dissipation ( automatically ) computed by LTspice is only 1/sfactor = 1/3 \nbecause simulation is done for only one phase !!!
TEXT -952 328 Left 2 ;Analysis of the network: \nSince the line_LF ln2 is very long ( 30 km ) relative to ln1 ( 1km )  the 120 kV voltage-slack v1\n takes nearly the full load ( v1_p = 116MW input )\nThe full load is 100Mw from the constpower load1 ( ld1_p ) and 20MW from the constcurrent Load2 ( i1_p ) \nThe 400kV voltage-slack v2 ( v2_p) contributes only 5 Mw\n \nExample 1 : To change the realpower flow you can change the transformer setting ( parameter s_ ). \nIf you makes s_ more positive -> realpower flows from the 400 kV side to the 120 kv side
TEXT -952 416 Left 2 ;Example 2: You want to decrease the 120 kV voltage ( t1_v1) : You can change the transformer setting ( parameter l_ )\nIf you make l_  more positive -> the voltage decrease on the 120kV side ( imagpower transfer to the 400 Kv side )\nAlternatively you can switch on a  l_batt or kd - element = KD1 ( inductance ) to the 120Kv side ( on=1 )\n \nExercise: Look at the imag power flow ( v2_q , ln2_q2, ln2_q1, t1_q2, t1_q1, kd_q, ln1_q1, ln1_q2, v1_q) and their influence to the voltage level\nHow much cappacitive power has the 30km - 400kV line_LF ?
